# Luminescent metal-organic frameworks for nitro explosives detection

Tingting Cheng1, Jinsong $\mathrm{Hu}^{1\ast}$ , Chunhui Zhou1, Yueming Wang1 & Mingdao Zhang2
1School of Chemical Engineering, Anhui University of Science and Technology, Huainan 232001, China 2Jiangsu Key Laboratory of Atmospheric Environment Monitoring and Pollution Control; School of Environmental Sciences and Engineering, Nanjing University of Information Science and Technology, Nanjing 210044, China
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been emerging as important multifunctional hybrid materials, not only due to the diversify framework architectures, but also contribute to the rich interactions among metals, ligands and guests. Nitro explosives have important influences for environmental protection and national homeland security. In this review, a brief description of luminescent MOFs is presented, accompanied by a short comment on the four types of metal-based luminescent MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection. Then the trends and challenges of luminescent MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives are also prospected.
## metal-organic frameworks, nitro explosives, luminescence, quench
Citation: Cheng T, Hu J, Zhou C, Wang Y, Zhang M. Luminescent metal-organic frameworks for nitro explosives detection. Sci China Chem, doi: 10.1007/s11426- 016-0061-5
## 1   Introduction
In the past two decades, the rapidly evolved multifunctional metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted great interests due to their extensive applications in gas adsorption [1a,1b], separation [1c], light emitting [1d], biomedical imaging [1e], chemical sensing [1f,1g], and heterogeneous catalysis [1h,1i]. Among the various potential applications, the chemical fluorescence sensing of MOFs have received growing attention, due to their easy operability, reusability, rapid response, high sensitivity and selectivity advantages [2]. Recently, a wide range of MOF-based sensors have been developed for the detection of cations [3a,3b], anion [3c,3d], small molecular organics [3e] and even biomacromolecules [3f]. There are many reasons for MOFs attracted great attentions as sensing materials: (1) their structural and chemical tunability can afford good selectivity through pore sieving functions with different pore sizes or host framework-guest interactions; (2) MOFs with high internal surface areas can concentrate analytes to high density, thereby decreasing detection limits and exhibiting high sensitivity. In addition, the striking features of MOFs have the ability to remove polluting guest small molecules as adsorbents. Therefore, MOFs have sparked a large intersts towards research on fluorescent sensing materials.
Nitro explosives have become a major threat to mankind health and national homeland security. Meanwhile, sorts of nitro explosives pollutions existed in the environment also prompted the scientific community to develop new detection methods. The chemical structures of common nitro explosive molecules are listed in Scheme 1. In response, recently, new ways to detect nitro explosives are being developed, such as gas chromatography coupled with mass spectrometry, Raman spectroscopy, neutron activation analysis, X-ray imaging and ion mobility spectroscopy (IMS) [4]. These instrumental techniques are highly selectivity and accuracy, however, great limited due to many shortcomings, such as low portability, complex pretreatments, and expensive instruments. Fortunately, recent studies showed that the fluorimetric method based on luminescence MOFs as probes has unique advantages, such as nondestructive detection, high sensitivity, fast response time, and real-time monitoring. In 2009, Li et al. [5] reported the first example of a MOF material that used to detect trace amounts of explosives (2,4-DNT and DMNB) in the vapor phase. Since then, more and more attentions have been put into nitro explosives detection.
## 2   Luminescence MOF materials and detection mechanisms
Luminescence can be defined as the emission of light upon absorption of energy under the condition that the energy source [6]. There are two basic types of luminescence: florescence, which is spin-allowed and has typical lifetimes on the order of 1 ns; and phosphorescence, which is spin-forbidden and has lifetimes that can be as long as several seconds [7]. The first reports of luminescence MOF appeared in 2002. Since then, more and more articles have appeared reporting light emission by MOFs, and a few reviews covering certain aspects of MOF luminescent properties have been published [7]. Metal-organic frameworks are combination of both inorganic and organic moieties within a single framework matrix allows systematic variation of fluorescent emissions by tunings different parameters such as metal ions, organic linkers or guest molecules [8]. Luminescene in MOFs generally arises from the building components: conjugated organic ligands or metal ions or clusters, although in some cases absorbed guest molecules may also contribute to the emission. The ability of luminescent MOFs to propagate the host-guest interaction to detectable changes as a function of their luminescence makes them promising candidates for sensor materials. A variety of electron transitions is possible in a MOF lattice, including transitions between molecular energy levels of linker molecules or molecules encapsulated in the MOF pores. All kinds of charge transfer processes were be shown here: ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT), metal-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT), ligand-to-ligand charge transfer (LLCT), metal-to-metal charge transfer (MMCT) and transitions between energy levels on specific metal ions (e.g. f-f transitions on rare earth metal ions, d-d transitions on transition metal ions) (Figure 1) [9]. Each single luminescence process can be influenced by chemical interaction, resulting in an emission “turned-on” or “turned-off”, different intensities, or color shiftes of the emission. As the interaction between MOF and nitro explosives, the electron-transfer will be found due to the electron-deficient of nitro explosives interact with electron rich of MOFs, result the reduction of the luminescence intensity by quenching leading to a “turn-off” effect of the quenching (Figure 2) [10]. Quantitative evaluation of luminescence MOF materials for a given analyte in solution can be achieved using Stern-Volmer analysis [11]:
$$
I_{\mathrm{o}}/I=1\mathrm{+}K_{\mathrm{sv}}[\mathrm{Q}]
$$
where $I_{0}$ and $I$ are the fluorescent intensities of the MOF material before and after the addition  of the analyte,  [Q] is  the concentration of the analyte, $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ is the Stern-Volmer constant. The higher $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ value indicates higher efficiency of the sensor. In recently years, a number of articles with the MOFs materials as sensor for detecting nitro explosives were published. There are various metals using in luminescent MOF materials, here we mainly summarize four types of metal-based luminescent MOF materials as sensor for detecting nitro explosives.
## 3   Transition metal-based luminescent MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
Optical detection methods are attracting increasing attention because of their high simplicity, sensiting and quick response time [12]. Most of the reported MOF-based sensors are made of transition metal constructed with aromatic ligands, as a main principle for the detection of electron-poor nitro explosives. MOF materials constructed with $\pi$ -electron rich ligands provide a suitably confined environment for efficient excition migration between electron-rich ligands and nitro explosives as electron-poor guests establishing new quenching pathways. As we known, the luminescent MOFs with $\mathrm{d}^{10}$ metal ions were potential candidates due to their excellent photoactive materials, so a great number of luminescent MOFs based znic and cadmium were reported.
## 3.1   Znic-based MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
As $\mathrm{d}^{10}$ metal ions, Zinc(II) MOFs were chosen as the sensing materials because of their high complex affinity to coordinated atoms and nondetrimental fluorescence. Mukherjeel et al. [13] reported a 3D $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -MOF: $[Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{(cpi)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3}]\cdot3D M A\cdot3E t O H\cdot6H_{2}O}$ by using a $\pi$ -conjugated ligand $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{cpi}$ . Studies showed that the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -MOF exhibited strong fluorescence peak at $470~\mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation at 350 nm while it dispersed in ethanol. When nitro explosive molecules such as NB, NT, NP, DNT and TNT get into the system, the fluorescence intensity was significant quenching (Figure 3). Sun et al. [14] utilized a flexible tridentated $\mathrm{ccmpb}^{3-}$ and bidentated bib building blocks successfully got two Zn-MOFs: $\mathrm{Zn_{3}(c c m p b)_{2}(b i b)\cdot3.6D M F\cdot4.0H_{2}O}$ (1) and $Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{ccmpb)}_{2}(\mathrm{bib)}$ (2). Studies showed that both of them can sensitively detect a very small amount of NB ( $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ in DMF) through noticeable fluorescence quenching, demonstrating extremely high detection sensitivities towards NB (Figure 4). A luminescent MOF, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{TDPAT})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]$ , with a dual functional luminescent sensor for quantitatively detecting the concentration of NB was successfully synthesized by Shi and his colleagues [15]. The complex exhibits strong luminescence in the solid state, the luminescence quantum yield is 0.213 due to the TDPAT6– has highly conjugated systems, which could act as the source of luminescence. The luminescence intensities decrease monotonically with an increase in the concentrations of NB, the limit of detection is down to $50\ \mathrm{\ppm}$ (Figure 5). Li and his colleagues [16] synthesized a luminescence MOF, $[\mathrm{Zn(MCA–OH)_{2}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy)0.5}]{\cdot0.5\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}{\cdot H_{2}O}}},$ with $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups on the pore surfaces for the first time. Studies showed that the MOF can be used for the detection of NB molecule via fluorescence quenching mechanism. Nagaraja and co-workers [17] reported a 5-fold interpenetrating luminescent MOF: $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(f d c)_{2}(b p e e)_{2}(H_{2}O)]}_{n}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The MOF shows highly selective sensing of NB via the fluorescence quenching mechanism. As shown in Figure 6, the fluorescence intensity reduces gradually as the concentration of NB increases, it was almost completely quenched at $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ of NB.
There are many articles reported that $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs materials can detect multinitro-explosives, such as 2,6-DNT, 2,4-DNT, TNT. However, little attention has been paid to the sensitive and selective detection of PA. Wang et al. [18] designed a dicarboxylic ligand $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}P D A}$ with the fragment of 9-phenylcarbazole, which exhibits excellent light emission and electron-donating properties, then they synthesized an electron-rich luminescent MOF: Zn-PDA. The MOF displays high selectivity in the detection of PA through synergistic effects including electron transfer, hydrogen-bonding and size matching. The Stern-Volmer constant $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ was determined to be $44502~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ by fitting the liner plot to the Stern-Volmer equation. Thus, this supramolecular synergistic tactic can detect PA effectively with less interference and improving application range in real application (Figure 7). Zheng et al. [19] also reported two luminescent MOFs, $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{tddb})_{2}(\mathrm{dpyb})]$ and $\mathrm{[Zn(tddb)_{2}(d i p b)]{\cdot}(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ , both of which exhibit highly selective for PA with the quenching constant $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ at $2.0{\times}10^{4}$ and $2.46{\times}10^{4}~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ , respectively. Konar et al. [20] utilized a $\pi$ -electron-rich tetra-topic carboxylate ligand H4tcpb successfully got a luminescent MOF: $\mathrm{[Zn(C_{34}H_{18}O_{8})_{0.5}(C_{20}N_{2}H_{16})_{0.5}]}\cdot\mathrm{[0.5(C_{20}N_{2}H_{16})\cdot2H_{2}O]}$ . The photoluminescence spectrum exhibits a strong fluorescence band at $415\ \mathrm{nm}$ with a small shoulder at $393~\mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation at $340~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is attributed to the LLCT between free and coordinated bpeb. The luminescent properties were further studied by dispersing in various nitro explosive molecules such as NB, 1,2-DNB, 1,3-DNB, 1,4-DNB, 4-NT, 2,4-DNT, 2,6-DNT and PA (Figure 8(c)). The PL intensity was dependent on the concentration of PA by quenching the initial fluorescence intensity by $94\%$ after adding $15.2\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of PA (Figure 8(a)). In addition, upon gradual addition of PA to the solution, an immediate color change from colorless to pale yellow was observed, while other analytes no considerable color change was noticed (Figure 8(b)). The quenching constant for PA was up to be $8.1{\times}10^{4}~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ (Figure 8(d)), which indicates the super quenching ability of the complex towards PA.
The most detections of nitro explosives were accomplished in solution, in fact, it is very important to detect in the vapor phase. Li et al. [21] discovered that $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{3}$ (bpy)]·4DMF· H2O, as one of the microporous metal organic frameworks. The MOF demonstrated fast, highly sensitive, and reversible detection of nitro explosives molecules in the vapor phase by the fluorescence quenching process. Parkin and co-workers [22] synthesized a fluorescent MOF $[\mathrm{Zn(dcbpy)(DMF)}]{\cdot}\mathrm{DMF}$ for the vapour-phase detection of nitro explosives. The quenching percentages were found to follow the order of DMNB $(11.5\%){>}\mathrm{NB}$ $(10.9\%){>}p{\cdot}\mathrm{NT}$ $(9.2\%)7{,}2{,}4$ -DNT $(7.9\%)$ for 300 s of exposure. Li et al. [23] reported two structurally related and dynamic MOFs: $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(n d c)_{2}(b p e)]}{\cdot2.5\mathrm{DMF}}{\cdot0.25\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpe})]{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}~0.5\mathrm{F}$ $0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , exhibiting 2D signal responses toward analytes in the vapor phase, such as TNT, RDX and DMNB (Figure 9). Then they also reported a lumiscence MOF-202: $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{hfdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy}){\cdot}x\mathrm{DMA}]$ , which exhibits  fast and  effective  sensing of RDX  via instant and selective detection of ketone vapors.
Sometimes, the practical response is always not very specific in terms of other nitro explosives making it quite difficult to identify and to detect individual species rather than just certain functionalities such as nitro group in a series of molecules. Supramolecular wire effect was utilized for improve detection of nitro explosives. A 2D MOF $\begin{array}{r}{[Z\mathrm{n}_{1.5}(\mathrm{TCPB}_{25})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})].}\end{array}$ it was synthesized with a starfish like electron rich ligand $\mathrm{{H_{3}T C P B}}$ (Figure 10). Quenching effects are based on several factors such as electron-rich and deficient areas of host and guest, vapor pressures, sizes and possible orientation for suitable interactions and others. Thus, high efficiency for both aromatic and aliphatic nitro explosive molecules was observed (Figure 11) [24].
## 3.2   Cadmium-based MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
Besides Zinc-based luminescence MOFs, Cd(II)-MOFs have also attracted intensive interests for their potential prospects in chemical sensors, photochemistry, and electroluminescent display [25]. Ma et al. [26] reported a novel anionic-MOF: $[(\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{NH_{2}}]_{4}[\mathrm{Cd_{3}}(\mathrm{H_{2phhc}})]$ . The emission peak at 446 nm $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}{=}348\ \mathrm{nm})$ is similar to that of H12phhc $\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}{=}440$ nm), originates from the intraligangd fluorescent emission of the H2phhc. Compare with other organic solvents, the NB emulsion displays the more obvious quenching effection (Figure 12). Therefore, the excited state electrons can transfer from MOF to NB, which leads to luminescence quenching. Studies showed that the luminescence intensity is nearly completely quenched with a high quenching efficiency of $92\%$ (at a concentration of $1000\mathrm{ppm}$ ).
As the “supramolecular wire effect” above, Zhu et al. [27] synthesized a microporous MOF $\mathrm{[Cd_{3}(T T P B)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{6}]}$ ·
6DMF based on a semirigid tripodal ligand with tetrazole groups $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{TTPB}$ . Studies showed that the MOF can be used for the detection of high explosives via supramolecular wire effect and the $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ was $2.2{\times}10^{2}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for NB. Recently, Sun et al. [28] reported a highly stable MOF luminescent switch, $\mathrm{[Cd_{3}(h c o o)(b i p y)_{2}\cdot4D M A]}_{n}$ , which exhibits clear fluorescence enhancement and “turn-off” quenching responses for benzene and NB vapors, which has high selectivity and sensitivity, and could be fully reusable (Figure 13). Lan and co-workers [29] reported a 2D luminecence MOF, $\mathrm{\small{[Cd_{2}C l(H_{2}O)(n t b)]}\cdot4.5D M A(N E N}$ U-503), high fluorescence quenching was monitored by PL spectroscopy when analytes such as 1,3-DNB, 2,4-DNT, NB, TNT and TNP were added to DMA. Studies showed that NENU-503 displays the most effective detection for NB. Interestingly, the color of the solution turned orange upon addition of TNT, and could be observed by the naked eye (Figure 14). Thus, the MOF can detect TNT with selectivity just by registering the color change, which is extremely convenient and fast.
Among these nitro based explosives, TNP (or PA) has higher explosive power than TNT, was commonly used in dyes, fireworks, matches, glass, and leather industries. So it is very important for the selective and sensitive detection of TNP present in soil and ground water. Su and co-workers [30] reported a Cd(II) MOF, $\mathrm{\small{[Cd_{3}(N T B)_{2}(D M A)_{3}]\cdot2D M A}}$ , which exhibits high sensitivity for TNP with $K_{\mathrm{sv}}{=}2.0{\times}10^{4}$ $\mathbf{M}^{-1}$ through luminescence quenching experiments. Koner et al. [31] reported a 3D luminescent supramolecular framework Cd-MOF: $\mathrm{[Cd}_{5}(\mathrm{TCA})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ . The Cd-MOF was found to be an interesting candidate for selective detection of TNP with the quenching constant $K_{\mathrm{sv}}{=}5.9{\times}10^{4}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ .
MOF detective materials of nitro explosives exhibit fast, highly sensitive, and reversible. However, there are few reports about the selective detection one nitro explosive molecules mixed nitro explosives (Figure 15) [32]. Ghosh et al. [32] reported the selective detection of the TNP by a fluorescent MOF, $[\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NDC})_{0.5}(\mathrm{PCA})]{\cdot}\mathrm{G}_{x},$ even in the presence of other nitro explosives in both aquenous and organic solution. Studies showed that the $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ was $3.5{\times}10^{4}~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for TNP in MeCN (Figure 16). Chang and co-workers [33] synthesized a 3D metal-organic framework: $[\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}]_{2}[\mathrm{Cd}_{17}(\mathrm{TCMM})_{12}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}.$ - $(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ ·solvents. The MOF shows high sensitivity and quick response toward the presence of trace amount of NB in solution and vapor state, which might be used for NB sensing application. The emission intensity was nearly completely quenched at a concentration of $100~\mathrm{ppm}$ in DMF, with a high quenching efficiency of $92.5\%$ and a low detection limit as low as $0.135~\mathrm{ppm}$ . Most important, this MOF shows outstanding anti-interference ability and could be used for sensing in the systems with complicated components (Figure 17).
## 3.3   Others transition metal-based MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
Though Cd-MOFs displayed high selectivity in the detection of nitro explosives, Cadmium is not the best metal from the viewpoint of green chemistry. So many attentions were transfered into other transition metal light emission MOFs. Berke et al. [34a] reported a Co-MOF, $\mathrm{\small{[Co_{2}(t t c)(D M F)(H_{2}O)]}}$ , which as TNP sensors with the $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ values obtained $5.2\times10^{4}$ $\mathbf{M}^{-1}$ .  Cui et al. [34b] has  got a 3D $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ coordination
polymer, $\mathrm{[Cu_{4}B r(C N)(m t z)}_{2}]_{n}$ which exhibits a highly sensitive naked-eye colorimetric sensor for NB and 2-NT. Mukherjee et al. [34c] reported a $\mathrm{Pt}2^{\mathrm{(II)}}$ -organometallic $180^{\circ}$ acceptor, $4^{\prime}$ -bis[trans-Pt(PEt3)2- $(\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{SCF}_{3})$ (ethynyl)]biphenyl and its $[2+2]$ self-assembly with three different ditopic “clip” type donors (La–Lc) to afford rectangular metallamacrocycles $\pmb{2}\mathbf{a}\mathrm{-}2\mathbf{c}$ (Figure 18). Upon gradual addition of PA to a $\mathrm{CHCl_{3}-C H_{3}O H}$ (1:1) solution of the macrocycle 2c, the fluorescence intensity was quenched rapidly. A linear Stern-Volmer plot was obtained from the fluorescence quenching titration profile, and the $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ is $5.0{\times}10^{6}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ . They further carried out the fluorescence quenching titration with PA at parts per billion (ppb) concentrations. The result shows that this particular macrocycle can sense the presence of PA even at the ppb level of concentration.
Ghosh et al. [35a] synthesized a Zr-MOF: $\mathrm{{.}Z r_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(p p\mathrm{{-}}}$ $\mathrm{dc})_{6}$ , the MOF is composed of non- $.Z\mathrm{r(IV)}$ metal centres and remains highly stable in water, the sizes of the pore windows is 11.5 and $23\textup{\AA}$ , which are larger than the size of the analytes, could permit easy diffusion of analytes inside the MOF, keeping the electron rich MOF and electron deficiene nitro analytes in close proximity (Figure 19). Studies showed that nitro analytes such as TNP, TNT, 2,4-DNT, could change the fluorescent intensity of the guest free MOF′ dispersed in water. Fluorescence quenching can be clearly observed for TNP concentrations of as low as $2.6~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , while all of the other nitro analytes had minor effects on the fluorescence intensity of MOF′ (Figure 20(a)). The fitting of the Stren-Volmer plot for TNP gave the quenching constant of $2.9{\times}10^{4}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ . This complex demonstrates highly selective and sensitive detection of TNP in aqueous media even in the persence of competing
nitro analytes (Figure 20(b)). Then Ghosh et al. [35b] further synthesized a 3D MOF matrix: $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{atdc})_{6}$ (UiO$68(\mathcal{W}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ with a pendant amine functionality for selective sensing of TNP in the aqueous phase. The guest-accessible Lewis basic amine functionalities can act as recognition sites for TNP via ionic and hydrogen-bonding interactions (Figure 21), the MOF can recognize TNP at as low as 0.4 ppm concentration. Meanwhile, the MOF-coated paper strip provides an effective and efficient way to detect TNP in the aqueous phase and demonstrate the potential of the MOF for real time in-field for sensing applications (Figure 22).
There are remain many transitions metal-based MOFs for luminescent sensing nitro explosives have been reported, and detection mechanisms were studied detailed, which were shown in Table 1.
## 4   Lanthanide-based luminescent MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
From above reports, transition metal based MOFs, specially Zinc and Cadmium-based luminescent MOFs, have emerged for as a type of material for the detection of nitro explosives using ligand centered luminescence [37]. However, the major disadvantage is the small stokes shift, which prevents the detection by naked eye as there is not much difference in color between  excitation light.  Fortunately,  the lanthanide based
MOFs, especially $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , opened up another direction of nitro explosives detection research based on their emission with large stoke shifts. So far, lanthanide MOFs focusing on photoluminescent properties have been reported extensively [38]. Most of the Ln-MOFs exhibit excellent luminescence properties, which could offer potential for the manufacture of luminescent probes.
## 4.1   Terbium-based MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
Qiu et al. [39] combined ultrasoud-vapour phase diffusion technique synthesize a luminescent MOF: $[\mathrm{Tb}(1,3,5{\cdot}\mathrm{BTC})]_{n}$ . This nanocrystal displayed a strong solid-state luminescence at 491, 546, and $589\mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation at $324~\mathrm{nm}$ at room temperature. Studies showed that the nanoscale Tb-based MOFs exhibit excellent luminescence properties for the highly selective and sensitive detection of TNP with the $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ values of $34182~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ . They also reported a magnetic metal-organic framework nanosphere, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\overline{{a}})$ Tb-BTC, which possess both magnetic characteristics and fluorescent properties using a layer by layer assembly technique (Figure 23).  The as-synthesized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\@$ Tb-BTC  nanospheres exhibit a remarkable fluorescent emission feature both in the suspension and in the solid state. As shown in Figure 24, strong fluorescence quenching effects were observed in all analytes, indicating high sensitivity for the detection of nitro explosives in ethanol, especially for TNT with the $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ values of $94800{\mathrm{M}}^{-1}$ . Meanwhile, the magnetic nanospheres can be easily recycled, which makes the MOF more convenient for reutilization and friendlier to the environment.
Liu et al. [40] reported a 2D layered Tb(Ⅲ)-MOF, $\mathrm{[Tb(Hbttc)(H_{2}O)_{4}]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ , which might be used for NB sensing application. The luminescence spectra with emission intensity of the MOF were completely quenched when the concentration of NB reached $769\mathrm{ppm}$ . Meantime, Liu et al. synthesized a Tb-MOF, $\mathrm{[Tb(tcpt)_{2/3}(T P A)_{1/2}(H_{2}O)]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ , exhibiting a 3D open framework structure. The luminescence of the MOF can be quenched by nitro explosive molecules, indicating its possibility of being a sensing material for nitro explosives (Figure 25). Mahata and co-worker [41] reported a submicron/nano sized metal-organic material: $[\mathrm{Y}_{2}(\mathrm{PDA})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{1}]_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1) and doping of $10\%$ terbium ions $(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+})$ in place of ${\mathrm{Y}}^{3+}$ afforded the desired phosphor material: $\mathbf{Tb}(\pmb{\mathscr{a}}\mathbf{1}$ . The dehydrated $\mathbf{Tb}@\mathbf{1}[\mathbf{Tb}@\mathbf{1}^{\prime}]$ shows a high intense visible green emission upon exposure to UV light. The luminescence of $\mathbf{Tb}(\pmb{\mathscr{a}},\mathbf{l}^{\prime}$ in acetonitrile shows high sensitivity and a quick visual detection ability toward the persence of a trace of amount of TNP, DNB, DNT and so on, via luminescence quenching. The schematic of the energy/electron transfer processed are shown in Figure 26. The quenching constants $(K_{\mathrm{sv}})$ to be $70920\ \mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for TNP, 44000 $\mathbf{M}^{-1}$ for DNB, $35430~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for DNT and $7690~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for NB (Figure 27 and Figure 28). The larger observed $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ values revealed extremely high sensitivity, indicated that $\mathbf{Tb}(\pmb{\omega}\mathbf{l}^{\prime}$ one of the best sensitive luminescence based metal-organic detectors of TNP, DNB and DNT.
## 4.2   Europium-based MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
Chen et al. [42a]  firstly  synthesized  nanoscale luminescent metal-organic framework, $\mathrm{Eu2(BDC)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}\cdot(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ for sensing of nitro explosives in ethanol solution. Zhou et al. [42b] based on ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{MFDA}}$ ligands, synthesized a highly efficient Eu-based luminescent MOF, $\mathrm{\Delta[Eu_{2}(M F D A)_{2}(H C O O)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{6}\cdot H_{2}O]}$ (1), with a quantum yield of $77\%$ . The emission of its dehydrated form 1b was almost completely quenched when the concentration of PA reached $1.0{\times}10^{-3}\mathrm{M}$ , showing that 1b as a turn-off luminescent sensory material for PA. Zuo and co-workers [42c] reported a microporous red light emitting MOF: $\mathrm{Eu_{3}(M F D A)_{4}(N O_{3})(D M F)_{3}}$ . Its solvent-free phase maintaining the identical framework structure shows efficient luminescence quenching phenomena for nitro compounds.
The linear Stren-Volmer relationships are all observed with $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of $4.59\times10^{3}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for 3,4-DNT, $1.98\times10^{3}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for 1,2-DNB and $1.31\times10^{3}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ for 2,4-DNT, indicate that the MOF is a potential luminescent sensory material for nitro explosives.
Zhou and co-workers [43] reported a highly efficient Eu-based luminescent MOF, $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{3}[\mathrm{Eu}_{3}(\mathrm{MHFDA})_{4}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{4}(\mathrm{D}-$ MF)2] $4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\cdot2{\mathrm{Me}}{\mathrm{CN}}$ , based on ( $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{MHFDA}_{,}^{\prime}$ . The MOF was heated at $100{-}170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a vacuum drying oven and then soaked in DMF to obtain $\mathrm{Eu_{3}(H_{0.75}M H F D A)_{4}(N O_{3})_{4}\cdot5.5D M F}$ (MOF-b). MOF-b exhibited red luminescence when excite at $340\ \mathrm{nm}$ , with characteristic emissions at 579, 591, and $614~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with shoulder peaks at 619, 652, and $700~\mathrm{nm}$ in the emission spectrum, corresponding to ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}{\longrightarrow}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{J}(J{=}0{-}4)$ transitions of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , respectively. The blue light emission in free ${\mathrm{H}_{2}}{\mathrm{MHFDA}}$ completely disappears and the ligands as light harvesting chromophores (antenna effect) effectively transfer energy to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions. Studies showed that the host of MOF-b provides the possibility of sensing target aromatic molecules via specific host-guest interactions (Figure 29). The MOF-b is a potential luminescent sensory material for nitro explosive molecules, especially for PA. The emission of MOF-b was almost completely quenched when the concentration of PA reached $1.0{\times}10^{-3}\mathrm{M}$ .
Recently, Song and co-workers [44a] selected $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ dfdc and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ to synthesize porous MOFs: $[\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{dfdc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{DEF})]_{n}$ and the solvent-free phase $[\mathrm{Eu(dfdc)}_{1.5}]_{n}$ , which can detect nitro explosives fast and sensitively, especially for the detection of TNP in DMF with $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ value of $6.24\times10^{4}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ . The high selectivity and high sensitivity of the fluorescence response of $[\mathrm{Eu(dfdc)}_{1.5}]_{n}$ to TNP indicate that the MOF could be used as an efficient fluorescence sensor for TNP. In addition to Terbium and Europium-based MOFs sensor materials, other Lanthanide-based luminescent MOFs are also reported. For example, Parkin et al. [44b] reported a highly fluorescent metal-organic frameworks- $\mathrm{\small{[Dy(dcbpy)(DMF)_{2}\cdot(N O_{3})]}}$ for the vapour-phase detection of nitro explosives.
## 5   Main group-based luminescent MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
## 5.1   P-region MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
Chen et al. [45a] has reported a photoluminescent microporpos metal organic anionic framework $[\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{tdmm})]$ $[\mathrm{NH_{2}(C H_{3})_{2}}]_{2}{\cdot}(\mathrm{DMF)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{16}}$ . The MOF exhibited a strong fluorescent emission peak at $360~\mathrm{nm}$ at room temperature, which is blue-shifted in comparison with the free ligand (420 nm). Meanwhile, the fluorescent emission of MOF showed very small solvent-dependence, the emission maximum being slightly blue-shifted when it were immersed into DMF $(349~\mathrm{nm})$ and EtOH $(348~\mathrm{nm})$ . The emission spectra were recorded upon immersion in DMF solutions of NB,1,3-DNB and 2,4-DNT. Studies showed that nitro explosive molecules could significantly quenched the luminescence of the MOF. As shown in Figure 30, the most effective quencher is NB and the least effective is 2,4-DNT. The maximum fluorescence intensity of the MOF was reduced by $81.5\%$ , $46.2\%$ and $14.0\%$ upon exposure to $5{\times}10^{-6}\mathrm{M}$ of NB, 1,3-DNB and 2,4-DNT, respectively. Zhu et al. [45b] reported a 3D porous aromatic framework (PAF-14) (Figure 31) with high fluorescence quantum yield was synthesized from luminescent monomer of TBPGe building blocks. As shown in Figure 32, different levels of quenching could be observed upon the addition of nitro explosive molecules such as NB, 2,4-DNT and TNT when dispersed PAF-14 in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ .
## 5.2   S-region MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
Huang et al. [46] reported a Mg-MOF: $\mathrm{\small{[Mg_{4}(T I A P y)(D M F)_{4}}}$ - $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{DMF})_{0.5}]$ constructed with the ligand $\mathrm{{H_{8}T I A P y}}$ . The maximum emissions of the ligand and complex are at 540 and $490\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The blue fluorescence shift is caused by metalto-ligand charge transfer (MLCT). The MOF show sensitive response to NB through a fluorescence quenching mechanism when dispersed in DMF. As shown in Figure 33, when the concentration of the NB was about $800~\mathrm{ppm}$ , the fluorescence was almost quenched completely. Meanwhile, this MOF could be regenerated and reused for a significant number of cycles by centrifuging the dispersed solution after NB detection and washing several times with fresh DMF. Moon et al. [47] reported a MOF $\mathrm{Li_{3}[L i(D M F)_{2}](C P M A)_{2}}\}$ ·4DMF $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ constructed with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{CPMA}$ , which has excellent stability (Figure 34). Studies showed that the maximum emission of MOF is at $430\mathrm{nm}$ . When the as-synthesized MOF absorbs NB molecule,  the obtained  MOF⊃NB  exhibits  a  deep-red color. Upon increasing concentration of NB in DMF, the maximum intensity of the fluorescence spectrum was decreased. The concentration for half-quenching of the fluorescence is calculated as $0.048\mathrm{M}$ of a NB/DMF solution. The detection sites are proven directly through single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformations, which show strong interactions between the aromatic rings of the electron-rich CPMA2– molecules and the electron-deficient NB.
6   Metalloligands-based and heterometalbased luminescent MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection
Instead of pure organic linkers in the construction of traditional MOFs, metalloligands as metal-containing complexes are widely utilized to further coordinate/bind with the second metal ions or metal clusters [48]. Luo et al. [49] reported an intensely  luminescent  heteronuclear  MOF $\{Z\mathrm{n}([\mathrm{Ir(ppy)}_{2}\cdot$ - $(\mathrm{dcbpy})_{2}]\cdot3\mathrm{DMF}\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , based on a highly light-harvesting dyclo-metalated iridium(III) unit. The complex exhibits efficient visible-light harvesting and strong visible yellow-orange emission with long lifetimes and high quantum yields and displays detection of nitro explosives on the ppm scale. They found that the addition of small amounts of the selected nitro explosives had a distinct quenching effect on the luminescence intensity of the dispersed complex in methanol. The quenching efficiency reached to $70\%$ at a concentration of $625\mathrm{ppm}$ for TNT, and the order of quenching efficiencies for the selected nitro compounds at the same concentration was TNT>DNT $\approx p$ -DNB ${\approx}m$ -DNB>NB (Figure 35).
At the same times, Su et al. [50] reported a 2D Cd(II)-lanthanide(III) heterometallic-organic frameworks: $\mathrm{[CdCl(L)Eu_{\itTb_{y}(H_{2}O)(D M A)](N O_{3})\cdot3D M A}}$ (IFMC-36- ${\mathrm{Eu}}_{x}{\mathrm{Tb}}_{y}$ , $\scriptstyle x=1$ , $y{=}0$ , IFMC-36-Eu). Studies showed that the photoluminescence intensity of IFMC-36-Eu is largely dependent on the solvent molecules (Figure 36), in which the physical interactions between the solute and solvent play a vital role. Notably, the PL intensity exhibits significant quenching behaviors especially in the NB. The luminescence intensity of the IFMC-36-Eu suspension significantly decreases into $55.7\%$ at a NB concentration of only $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 37(a)), and almost completely quenches at $120~\mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 37(b)). As shown in Figure 38, IFMC-36-Eu shows outstanding anti-inerference ability and could be applied as a fluorescence sensor for NB with high sensitivity and selectivity in the systems with complicated components.
## 7   Summary and perspective
According to the current research status, a large of progresses have been obtained for luminenscent MOFs as nitro explosives sensor material, there is still some questions should be resolved.  One is  selective detection  in aqueous  media and vapour state. For in-field selective detection of nitro explosive molecules present in soild, ground water and air, probe working in aqueous media or vapour is highly desirable. Although luminenscent MOFs sensing materials have been employed for liquid, there has little MOF based probes which can selectively detect nitro explosives in aqueous media or vapors. Another is repeatability and operability. The luminenscent MOFs sensing materials must be rapid and convenient recovery. This is very important for practical application to design the material to be a molecular device or film by chemical and physical methods, which will effectively improve its operability and became one of the important issues in near the further. Finally, the majority of the research so far has been focused on single luminescence metal-organic framework, but with an increased attention on metalloligands-based and heterometal-based luminescent MOFs. The immobilization of photoactive molecular complex with an extended framework is expressly helpful for designing new optical sensors with designated properties.
This review illustrates rapid development of luminenscent MOFs applied to nitro explosives detection. The emission wavelength, the luminescence properties and quantum yield of the luminenscent MOF sensing materials can be changed, mainly due to the interaction between analyte molecules and metal ions or ligands. At present, although the luminenscent MOFs as sensing materials for nitro explosives detection have made a great progress at home and abroad studies, it has still a long road to go for practical applications. We certainly believe that luminescent MOFs have a bright future for nitro explosives detection.
